
[[futhark]]
=== Futhark

==== Sourcing Futhark functions

Futhark has no pool of its own. Morloc compiles each sourced `.fut` file with
the Futhark compiler and links it into the {cpp} pool, so a call between
Futhark and {cpp} code does not cross a pool. You source a Futhark `entry` the
way you source any other function:

[source, morloc]
.main.loc
----
module main (scaledSum)

import root-cpp
import vector

source Futhark from "kernels.fut" ("scaled_sum" as scaledSum)

scaledSum :: F32 -> Vector n F32 -> F32
----

[source]
.kernels.fut
----
entry scaled_sum [n] (s: f32) (v: [n]f32) : f32 =
  f32.sum (map (\x -> s * x) v)
----

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o demo main.loc
...
$ ./demo scaledSum 2.0 '[1,2,3,4]'
20
----

A Futhark signature may use only fixed-width numeric scalars, `Vector` and
`Matrix` of them, and tuples of those that do not nest. The build checks each
signature against the entry the Futhark compiler reports, so declaring `F64`
for this `f32` entry fails before anything runs:

----
Futhark entry scaled_sum: morloc signature implies f64 rank-0 but the entry has f32 rank-0
----

==== Futhark backends and GPU devices

Futhark code is compiled for a backend chosen with two build parameters
(<<build-parameters>>):

* `backend` -- the Futhark compilation backend: one of `c` (the default,
  sequential CPU), `multicore` (parallel CPU), `opencl`, `cuda`, `hip`, or
  `ispc`. The GPU backends (`opencl`, `cuda`, `hip`) are what make Futhark run on
  a GPU.
* `device` -- which device to run on, for a GPU backend, using Futhark's device
  selector syntax (`#0`, `#1`, ...). It is only valid together with a GPU
  backend.

[source, bash]
----
# build the Futhark kernels for CUDA and run on the second GPU
morloc make -X futhark:backend=cuda -X futhark:device=#1 -o nexus main.loc
----

A GPU build runs on the single selected device, and calls into the Futhark
context are serialized. The required GPU runtime libraries (for example
`-lcuda -lnvrtc` for CUDA) are linked automatically; if your toolchain lives in
a non-standard location, add the paths with the {cpp} escape hatch, e.g.
`-X cpp:flags=-L/usr/local/cuda/lib64`.


